parliament stepped up efforts to help  boost ukraine's economy as meps endorsed  a proposal to remove 98 of custom duties  that would save exporters and  manufacturers at 487 million euros a  year at a time when russia is blocking  ukrainian imports the help couldn't come  fast enough but for the mepu draft of  the report ukraine will quickly need to  sign up to a free trade agreement with  the eu once these temporary measures of  assistance run out these measures which  are voted today are temporary measures  they should last not longer than until  the 1st of november why  because we expect that after the  presidential elections in ukraine in may  in june we will be able to sign this  association agreement  it's hoped that revising the rules and  clinical trials will cut the red tape  and put an end to trend that has seen  trials outsourced to china and asia one  of the main changes is that academics  and big pharmaceuticals will have to  upload the results of all trials onto a  public database but we've made it clear  that once they've asked for market  authorisation for a medicine it's no  longer commercially confidential that  means that we'll get the details of all  trials published whether they're  successful or whether they're not  successful and that's really important  for good medicine and it's good for  people's trust in medicine as well  it's become the norm to use debit or  credit cards to make online payments for  goods or services cashless transactions  are fast and easy but costly and fees  vary across member states with elections  around the corner another dossier that's  been pushed through before the end of  the parliamentary term will cap credit  card fees to 0.3 percent and debit card  transactions to 0.2 percent it was  always a good deal between well the more  or less the two  credit card companies mastercard visa  and the banks  so those were the two deciding on those  fees so they were far higher than needed  so of course the banks will now 